1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
Ideas die slowly when you refuse to decide.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:07,141
Write a kill criterion early so you do not romanticize a dead offer.

3
00:00:07,141 --> 00:00:09,521
This lesson is about kill criteria early.

4
00:00:09,521 --> 00:00:11,562
When to change offer or audience.

5
00:00:11,562 --> 00:00:13,942
Do not treat this as theory only.

6
00:00:13,942 --> 00:00:17,343
Treat it as a move you can run this week.

7
00:00:17,343 --> 00:00:22,443
By the end, you should be able to act without waiting for a perfect plan.

8
00:00:22,443 --> 00:00:26,184
Keep the DigProg habit: watch, practice, then take one real action.

9
00:00:26,504 --> 00:00:29,503
A good kill criterion is specific and time-boxed.

10
00:00:29,503 --> 00:00:37,375
Example: if I cannot get three real conversations about this offer in fourteen days, I change the audience or the offer.

11
00:00:37,375 --> 00:00:40,374
Remember the objective: write a 14-day kill criterion.

12
00:00:40,374 --> 00:00:44,498
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

13
00:00:44,498 --> 00:00:48,996
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

14
00:00:48,996 --> 00:00:53,120
Avoid vanity work that feels busy but does not create proof.

15
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:55,412
Kill criteria protect your time.

16
00:00:55,412 --> 00:00:56,595
Romance is optional.

17
00:00:56,595 --> 00:00:57,779
Evidence is not.

18
